13:47:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I see exactly the logic you are using here. If someone joins a specific political party, signs a contract, or joins a club, we generally assume they are endorsing the entire platform. It makes complete logical sense to apply that same standard to a religion: if the text and the history contain violence, then claiming the religion means you are endorsing that violence. The reason experts and sociologists do not apply this logic to individual Muslims is because human beings do not interact with ancient world religions the same way they interact with modern political ideologies or contracts. Here is why claiming a religion is not the same as "buying into the whole package" of its most violent historical or textual elements: **1. The "Terms of Service" Fallacy** No ancient world religion is practiced as a 100% literal, unyielding endorsement of every single line of its texts or every action of its historical founders. If we apply the "whole package" rule universally, we would have to look at Christianity and Judaism the same way. The Bible contains explicit rules for how to buy and beat slaves (Exodus 21), commands to stone people to death for working on the Sabbath (Numbers 15), and commands from God to slaughter entire cities of men, women, and children (1 Samuel 15). When a modern Christian or Jew goes to church or synagogue, we do not assume they are "buying into" slavery or the slaughter of Canaanites. We understand that they are focusing on the spiritual core of their faith—love, charity, community, and moral guidance—while viewing the violent parts as ancient history or contextualizing them through modern theology. Everyday Muslims do the exact same thing with the Quran and Islamic history. **2. What the "Package" Actually Looks Like for Individuals** When an everyday Muslim practices their faith, the "package" they are buying into is not a 7th-century war manual. For the vast majority of Muslims, practicing the religion means adhering