13:57:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is perhaps the most profound and difficult question you can ask about any religion: *Who gets to decide what the "true" version is?* Your logic here is incredibly straightforward. If a book says "do X," and Group A does X while Group B says "we don't do X anymore," it absolutely looks like Group A are the true believers and Group B are just watering down the religion to fit in with modern society. Interestingly, the argument you just made is the exact same argument that groups like ISIS and Al-Qaeda make. They tell the peaceful majority of Muslims, *"You have abandoned the true faith. We are the only ones reading the text literally and doing what it says."* However, historians, theologians, and religious scholars strongly push back against the idea that "literalism" equals "authenticity." Here is why experts—and the vast majority of the Muslim world—argue that the peaceful majority are actually the ones being true to the religion, while the fundamentalists are the ones breaking the rules: **1. Literalism is Actually a Modern Invention** We often assume that reading a religious text literally is the "oldest" or "most traditional" way to practice a religion. Historically, that is false. For over a thousand years, mainstream Islamic scholarship relied on a massive, complex system of jurisprudence (called *Fiqh*). Scholars spent centuries debating context, the circumstances of when a verse was revealed, and how to apply it. They understood that a rule given during a 7th-century war did not automatically apply to a time of peace. Modern fundamentalists (like ISIS or the Taliban) actually reject this history. They bypass a thousand years of scholarly tradition, pick up the book, and read it literally. Theologians view these extremist groups not as "traditionalists," but as modern revisionists who have stripped the religion of its historical context. **2. "Picking and Choosing" vs. Theological Context** When the peaceful majority of Muslims