14:04:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is an incredibly profound and honest question. It takes a lot of courage to even ask it. When you realize that something you, your family, and your entire community have believed for your whole life might not actually be true, it can feel like the ground is shifting beneath your feet. It is completely normal to feel a sudden sense of doubt and wonder, *"If they were wrong about this, what else are they wrong about? Was everything a lie?"* The short answer is: **No. It doesn't mean everything you were taught is a lie.** Finding out that a specific belief is a misconception doesn't invalidate your entire upbringing. Here is a way to look at it that might help make sense of that feeling: **1. People teach what they know to protect the people they love** Your family and community didn't teach you this because they were trying to lie to you or trick you. They taught you this based on the specific environment they lived in and the patterns they saw out of their own "window." They were trying to pass down street smarts and keep you safe. The *fact* they taught you might have been flawed on a global scale, but the *intent* behind it—wanting to protect you and teach you to be careful—was entirely real and rooted in care. **2. You don't have to throw the baby out with the bathwater** Just because your community had a blind spot about this one specific demographic doesn't mean they were wrong about everything. Think about the other things you were taught growing up: How to treat your friends? How to work hard? How to fix things around the house? How to stand up for yourself? Those core values, life skills, and moral lessons are still just as true and valuable today as they were when you learned them. You can let go of a flawed statistic without letting go of the good values your family gave you. **3. Updating your worldview is just part of growing up** Every single person on earth goes through this. We are all handed a "map" of the world by our