14:11:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand why it feels that way. It is deeply unsettling to be told that a pattern you feel you’ve clearly observed might not be accurate. It makes total sense that you would think, "If I'm wrong about this, what else am I wrong about?" But I want to reassure you: pointing out that this specific generalization is inaccurate does not mean your entire life experience is invalid, or that your general judgment about people is broken. It is not an all-or-nothing situation. You can absolutely trust what you saw with your own eyes: you had tenants who left your property in terrible condition, and those specific tenants happened to be Hispanic. Your eyes didn't deceive you about the mess, the stress, or the financial cost you had to deal with. Those experiences are 100% real. Where human judgment often trips up—for all of us, not just you—is in how we draw broad conclusions from our own limited, personal experiences. Think about it like this: If you flip a coin five times and it lands on tails every time, your eyes are telling you the truth. It really did land on tails five times. But if you conclude from that experience that "this coin will always land on tails," that conclusion is flawed, because five flips is too small of a sample size to represent the true odds. As an individual landlord, you might rent to 10, 20, or even 50 people over a lifetime. That is a very small sample size compared to the millions of Hispanic people in the world. If you happen to get a few bad tenants who share a demographic, your brain naturally connects the dots—especially if your family has told you to look out for those exact dots. But those few individuals do not represent the habits of an entire diverse population. You don't have to throw away your life experience or stop trusting your judgment. You just have to recognize the limits of personal experience when it comes to judging millions of people you haven't met. Trust your experience that bad tenants exist, and trust your judgment that protecting your property is vital. The best way to apply that judgment is to enforce strict, individual screening—like checking references and credit—for *every* single applicant. That allows you to use your life experience to protect yourself, without relying on inaccurate stereotypes.
