14:17:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: When you see a pattern repeating across different areas of your life—like moving from rental properties to the breakroom at work—it strongly reinforces the belief that the pattern is a universal rule. However, seeing this in the breakroom is actually a very clear example of how confirmation bias operates in our day-to-day lives. Because you grew up hearing this belief from your family, and because you had those highly stressful experiences with tenants, your brain has built a specific filter. Here is how that filter works in a shared space like a breakroom: If a coworker of a different background leaves a dirty microwave, spills coffee, or leaves trash behind, your brain likely just registers it as an individual annoyance. You think, "Bob is a slob," or "People are so disrespectful of shared spaces." You blame the individual person or the general office culture. But if a Hispanic coworker leaves that exact same mess, your brain's filter lights up. Instead of just thinking, "That coworker is a slob," your brain says, "See? There it is again. The pattern." You remember those specific instances vividly because they confirm the rule you were taught to look for. Meanwhile, the times when Hispanic coworkers clean up after themselves, or the times when coworkers of other backgrounds leave a mess, fade into the background because they don't fit the narrative. Shared spaces like work breakrooms are universally notorious for being messy. Ask anyone who works in an office, regardless of the demographic makeup of the staff, and they will likely complain about how their coworkers leave the kitchen. It is a well-documented human behavior—sometimes called the "tragedy of the commons"—where people generally take less care of shared spaces than they do their own personal spaces. The messes in the breakroom are real, and the annoyance you feel about them is completely justified. But a messy coworker is just a messy coworker. Attributing their lack of consideration to their ethnicity is the result of a cognitive filter, not an objective reality about that demographic.
